## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical memory management, lock-free data structures, and safe object reuse under contention. A beginner-friendly allocator emphasizes alignment and simple reclaim strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free freelist using CAS
- ABA avoidance (tagged pointers or hazard pointers)
- Slab-style pool with 64-byte blocks
- Cache-line awareness and fragmentation

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code for allocate/free
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;
typedef struct Pool { Block* head; void* pool; size_t block_size; } Pool;

Block* allocate(Pool* p){
  Block* old = atomic_load(&p->head);
  while (old){
    Block* next = old->next;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->head, &old, next)){
      return old;
    }
  }
  return NULL;
}
void deallocate(Pool* p, Block* b){
  Block* head = atomic_load(&p->head);
  do {
    b->next = head;
  } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->head, &head, b));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect pool exhaustion and recover?
- How would you extend to variable-size objects or per-object headers?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical concurrency on bare-metal MCUs: interrupt-safe data sharing, wrap-around handling, and overflow behavior in a tight, deterministic path.

## Key Concepts
- Circular buffers with power-of-two sizing
- Volatile indices and memory visibility between ISR and main code
- Minimal interrupt masking to avoid latency spikes
- Edge-case testing for wrap and overflow

## Code Example
```c
#define N 32
#define MASK (N-1)
static uint8_t buf[N];
static volatile uint8_t head = 0; // consumer index
static volatile uint8_t tail = 0; // producer index

int enqueue_byte(uint8_t b) {
  uint8_t next = (tail + 1) & MASK;
  if (next == head) return -1; // full
  buf[tail] = b;
  // memory barrier if available
  tail = next;
  return 0;
}

int dequeue_byte(uint8_t *out) {
  if (head == tail) return -1; // empty
  *out = buf[head];
  head = (head + 1) & MASK;
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for multi-producer/multi-consumer safely?
- What tests would you run on a simulated interrupt workload to prove correctness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests fundamentals of memory allocators, pointer arithmetic, alignment, and simple free-list management in a low-level setting. It also checks the ability to reason about initialization and fragmentation.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size allocator
- Free-list embedded pointers
- Heap initialization
- O(1) allocation/deallocation
- Fragmentation considerations with fixed-size pools

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>

#define HEAP_SIZE (64*1024)
#define BLOCK_SIZE 32
#define BLOCKS (HEAP_SIZE / BLOCK_SIZE)

static uint8_t heap[HEAP_SIZE];
static uint32_t free_head;

void init_heap(){
  free_head = 0;
  for(size_t i=0;i<BLOCKS;++i){
    uint32_t next = (i==BLOCKS-1) ? UINT32_MAX : (i+1);
    *((uint32_t*)(heap + i*BLOCK_SIZE)) = next;
  }
}

void* alloc_block(){
  if(free_head==UINT32_MAX) return NULL;
  uint32_t off = free_head;
  free_head = *((uint32_t*)(heap + off*BLOCK_SIZE));
  return (void*)(heap + off*BLOCK_SIZE);
}

void free_block(void* p){
  size_t off = ((uint8_t*)p - heap) / BLOCK_SIZE;
  *((uint32_t*)(heap + off*BLOCK_SIZE)) = free_head;
  free_head = off;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to multiple threads? Consider a per-thread arena or atomic free-list head.

## Why This Is Asked
Real systems synchronize thousands of threads across NUMA nodes; a lock-free barrier minimizes cache thrash and jitter in schedulers and regression tests.

## Key Concepts
- Generation counters with per-thread sense flips
- Atomic arrivals and a single release owner
- Cache-line padding to avoid false sharing
- Timeout handling and barrier reset
- NUMA locality considerations and fairness

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  alignas(64) volatile int gen;
  alignas(64) volatile int arrivals;
  char pad[60];
} barrier_t;

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b, int tid, int total, int timeout_ms) {
  int g = __atomic_load_n(&b->gen, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
  if (__atomic_fetch_add(&b->arrivals, 1, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST) == total-1) {
    __atomic_store_n(&b->arrivals, 0, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
    __atomic_store_n(&b->gen, g+1, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
  } else {
    while (__atomic_load_n(&b->gen, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST) == g) {
      _mm_pause();
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for dynamic thread counts?
- What are the trade-offs vs futex-based barriers?
- How would you test for liveness under shutdowns and timeouts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests low-level memory management, determinism, and fragmentation handling in tight constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool vs variable allocations
- In-block free-list approach
- Alignment and fragmentation
- Initialization, allocation, freeing, and testing

## Code Example

```c
/* Initialization and simple allocator skeleton */
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;

static uint8_t pool[4096];
static Block* free_list;

void pool_init(void){
  free_list = (Block*)pool;
  Block* cur = free_list;
  for (size_t i=0; i< (4096/32)-1; ++i){
    cur->next = (Block*)((uint8_t*)cur + 32);
    cur = cur->next;
  }
  cur->next = NULL;
}
void* pool_alloc(size_t s){
  if (s>32) return NULL;
  if (!free_list) return NULL;
  Block* b = free_list;
  free_list = b->next;
  return (void*)b;
}
void pool_free(void* p){
  ((Block*)p)->next = free_list;
  free_list = (Block*)p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you add support for variable-sized allocations with splitting and coalescing?
- How would you verify correctness with a tiny unit test harness on a microcontroller?

